 ## Overview
-Spatial Indexing and Compound Ordering maps $N$-dimensional data (spatial coordinates plus discrete non-spatial dimensions like time or channel) into a 1D linear array to optimize cache locality and network fetching. It solves the problem of poor spatial coherence in multidimensional datasets by sorting elements first by discrete non-spatial coordinates, and then by a space-filling curve (SFC) within each discrete slice [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard Morton or Hilbert encoding which only handles spatial coordinates, this method hierarchically structures the data so that contiguous memory blocks correspond to both spatial proximity and discrete slice membership, enabling efficient range queries for specific time steps or channels [S1, unofficial].
